  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}=5(x-4)+\\frac{{5}^{2}(x-4)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{3}(x-4)^{3}}{3!}+\u22ef\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{5}^{n+1}(x-4)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{5}^{n}(x-4)^{n}}| \\\\  & =5|x-4|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =5|x-4|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
